---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { sitePath } from "../lib/path.js";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Principles · Bootstrap HTML"
  description="Why Bootstrap HTML prefers native HTML, progressive enhancement, platform accessibility, and machine-readable contracts for AI."
>
  <h1>Principles</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Bootstrap 5.3.8 look and tokens, with behavior from the platform instead of
    <code>bootstrap.js</code>. This page is the why. Install and migration live under
    <a href={sitePath("/getting-started")}>Getting started</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 class="h4" id="what-this-is">What this is</h2>
  <p>
    Class names stay Bootstrap’s. Widgets open and close with native HTML:
    <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>,
    <code>popover</code>,
    <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>, and
    <code>command</code> /
    <code>commandfor</code>.
    There is no
    <code>data-bs-*</code>
    attribute layer and no classList-driven UI state.
  </p>
  <p>
    The
    <a href={sitePath("/")}>Overview</a>
    lists every component and what works without script. This package is a
    preview: CSS-only Bootstrap behavior tracks 5.3.8; native widgets follow
    this package’s own versioning.
  </p>

  <h2 class="h4" id="principles">Principles</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Prefer native HTML and CSS over JavaScript. Fix layout with CSS. Do not
      scroll, measure, or reposition elements in script to correct layout.
    </li>
    <li>
      Target
      <a href="https://webstatus.dev/">Baseline Newly available</a>
      features. Experimental platform APIs are in scope when a component is
      marked experimental; they must degrade rather than break core use.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not load
      <code>bootstrap.js</code>
      or
      <code>bootstrap.bundle.js</code>.
      Optional
      <code>pe-*</code>
      behaviors exist only when the platform has no CSS path for the job.
    </li>
    <li>
      Polyfills live under
      <code>src/polyfills/</code>
      and load only after a feature check, and only when the document uses the
      matching markup. Never load a polyfill unconditionally.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="h4" id="progressive-enhancement">Progressive enhancement</h2>
  <p>
    A dialog, disclosure, or popover works when the parser reaches it.
    Bootstrap’s plugins wait for
    <code>bootstrap.js</code>
    to download, parse, and attach listeners. That wait is Total Blocking Time
    on the main thread. These controls add no such task.
  </p>
  <p>
    Scripts enhance. Dismissing an alert, switching tabs, auto-hiding a toast,
    or clamping a dual range needs a small module on pages that use that
    markup. Without it, the visible control still works where the platform
    already does the job; the extra behavior is simply absent.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every page loads the polyfill loader. It checks the APIs and stops. A
    fallback downloads only when that API is missing and the page uses it. As
    browsers ship the feature, the file stops being requested. See
    <a href={sitePath("/polyfills")}>Polyfills</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some gaps have no polyfill. Anchor positioning is the common case: a
    dropdown or popover still opens when CSS Anchor Positioning is missing; it
    is just not tethered to the button. Prefer a working untethered menu over a
    dead control.
  </p>

  <h2 class="h4" id="accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
  <p>
    Use the platform control. A modal is a
    <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>,
    a collapse is
    <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>,
    a button toggle is a checkbox or radio with
    <code>.btn-check</code>.
    Do not reinvent roles and
    <code>aria-expanded</code>
    on top of an element that already exposes the state.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each component page ends with accessibility notes from the inventory. Name
    icon-only controls. Prefer
    <code>&lt;label for&gt;</code>
    over aria-only labeling when a visible label exists. Follow the avoid list:
    common mistakes are listed there so they are not reintroduced as “helpful”
    ARIA.
  </p>

  <h2 class="h4" id="ai">AI</h2>
  <p>
    Agents that only have the docs URL should start from the machine-readable
    contracts, not from scraping component pages for markup.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href={sitePath("/llms.txt")}>/llms.txt</a>
      — markup rules and absolute links to every component page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={sitePath("/llms-full.txt")}>/llms-full.txt</a>
      — each example in an
      <code>html</code>
      code block next to its avoid list.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={sitePath("/components.json")}>/components.json</a>
      — the same examples plus polyfills, browser support, and accessibility
      notes, as JSON for tooling.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The installed package ships matching files at
    <code>node_modules/bootstrap-html/dist/llms-full.txt</code>
    and
    <code>node_modules/bootstrap-html/dist/components.json</code>.
    Point a project’s agent instructions at those paths so generated markup
    starts from the example, follows the avoid list, and loads any
    <code>pe-*</code>
    module the contract names.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
